1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:11,693
On the first of July, 1916, at half past seven in the morning, whistles blew along the British front in northern France.

2
00:00:12,073 --> 00:00:18,189
By nightfall, the British Army had suffered more casualties than on any other day in its history.

3
00:00:18,569 --> 00:00:21,504
This is the story of the Battle of the Somme.

4
00:00:22,554 --> 00:00:29,714
By 1916, the Western Front was a line of trenches running from the Channel coast to the Swiss border.

5
00:00:30,094 --> 00:00:36,116
In December 1915, at Chantilly, the Allies agreed to strike together in the new year,

6
00:00:36,116 --> 00:00:40,366
with a great Anglo-French offensive astride the River Somme.

7
00:00:41,416 --> 00:00:44,776
But in February, Germany attacked the French at Verdun.

8
00:00:45,156 --> 00:00:51,012
As French divisions were drawn into that battle, the Somme became a mainly British offensive,

9
00:00:51,012 --> 00:00:54,412
with a new purpose: to relieve the pressure on Verdun.

10
00:00:55,462 --> 00:01:03,422
Britain's small professional army had been joined by hundreds of thousands of volunteers who answered Lord Kitchener's call.

11
00:01:03,802 --> 00:01:09,314
Many served in Pals battalions: friends, neighbours and workmates from the same towns,

12
00:01:09,314 --> 00:01:12,648
who had enlisted together, and would fight together.

13
00:01:13,697 --> 00:01:19,715
The plan, under Sir Douglas Haig and General Rawlinson's Fourth Army, rested on artillery.

14
00:01:20,095 --> 00:01:25,672
From the twenty-fourth of June, British guns fired more than one and a half million shells.

15
00:01:26,052 --> 00:01:33,176
It was not enough. Most were shrapnel, useless against deep dugouts, and as many as a third failed to explode.

16
00:01:33,556 --> 00:01:38,200
Much of the barbed wire survived. And the Germans waited, underground.

17
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:43,596
At twenty past seven, a mine exploded beneath Hawthorn Ridge.

18
00:01:43,976 --> 00:01:51,682
At seven twenty-eight, more mines tore open the German line, among them the vast Lochnagar mine at La Boisselle.

19
00:01:52,062 --> 00:01:59,927
At seven thirty, the infantry climbed out of their trenches, and the German machine gunners ran up from their dugouts to meet them.

20
00:02:00,977 --> 00:02:08,438
At Serre, the Accrington Pals lost 585 of about 720 men in half an hour.

21
00:02:08,818 --> 00:02:17,199
At Beaumont-Hamel, 801 men of the Newfoundland Regiment went forward. Only 68 answered the next roll call.

22
00:02:17,579 --> 00:02:24,132
In the south, British and French troops took most of their objectives, including Montauban and Mametz.

23
00:02:24,512 --> 00:02:34,186
But by nightfall, the British had suffered 57,470 casualties, 19,240 of them killed.

24
00:02:35,236 --> 00:02:39,832
There was no breakthrough. The battle became a grinding struggle of attrition.

25
00:02:40,212 --> 00:02:45,164
On the fourteenth of July, a surprise dawn attack took Bazentin Ridge.

26
00:02:45,544 --> 00:02:52,017
South Africans held Delville Wood for six days; of more than three thousand men,

27
00:02:52,017 --> 00:02:53,069
780 came out.

28
00:02:53,449 --> 00:02:59,290
At Pozières, the Australians suffered 23,000 casualties in under seven weeks.

29
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:06,335
On the fifteenth of September, at Flers-Courcelette, Britain unveiled a new weapon: the tank.

30
00:03:06,715 --> 00:03:12,286
Of forty-nine, thirty-two reached the start line, and only eighteen went into action.

31
00:03:12,286 --> 00:03:15,761
But one led the infantry up the main street of Flers.

32
00:03:16,811 --> 00:03:22,464
Thiepval, an objective for the first day, finally fell on the twenty-sixth of September.

33
00:03:22,844 --> 00:03:26,623
Then the rains came, and the battlefield dissolved into mud.

34
00:03:27,003 --> 00:03:32,455
Beaumont-Hamel was taken at last on the thirteenth of November. Five days later,

35
00:03:32,455 --> 00:03:35,453
in sleet and snow, the offensive was halted.

36
00:03:36,503 --> 00:03:41,721
In 141 days, the Allies had advanced no more than seven miles.

37
00:03:42,101 --> 00:03:49,694
The British Empire suffered some 420,000 casualties, the French about 200,000,

38
00:03:49,694 --> 00:03:52,907
and the Germans at least 450,000.

39
00:03:53,957 --> 00:04:00,883
Was it a futile slaughter, or a costly but necessary step towards victory? Historians still argue.

40
00:04:01,263 --> 00:04:10,211
The German army had been badly worn down. A German staff officer called the Somme the muddy grave of the German field army,

41
00:04:10,211 --> 00:04:14,066
and in early 1917 it withdrew to the Hindenburg Line.

42
00:04:14,446 --> 00:04:23,455
Today, the Thiepval Memorial bears the names of more than 72,000 British and South African men who have no known grave.

43
00:04:23,835 --> 00:04:28,464
The fields have long been ploughed. But every summer, the poppies return.
